+- `MergeWorker` + `FeedInsertWorker` stub: no gain (notifications 7.86 vs 7.90). Timelines still pass with `FeedInsert` stubbed (fallback to DB? `HomeFeed`?), but no time saved (few followers → few inserts). Skipped to avoid risk for timelines that might need feeds.
+- `User#send_devise_notification` (Devise confirmation emails via `AppSignUpService`): 5.4% in `accounts_spec` (`Devise::Confirmable#send_confirmation_instructions` → `User#send_devise_notification` → `render_and_send_devise_message` → `deliver_later` inline). Spot-test with prepend + AMS disable gave accounts 3.25→2.37 (-27% vs -20% without), but average across 12 files worsened slightly (24.16→25.22s, +4% variance/overhead). Only `POST /api/v1/accounts` specs trigger it (few files). Skipped to minimize risk (admin confirmations resend uses same path; though current test only checks empty case, future success-case tests would need tag).
+- `ActiveModelSerializers` instrumentation disable (`notify_render` + `tag_logger` → `yield`): `AMS::Logging` already unsubscribed (`config/initializers/active_model_serializers.rb` unsubscribes `RENDER_EVENT`), but `instrument` + `tagged` wrappers still cost ~10% total (accounts) / 13.9% (notifications) for every serialization (webhook + API responses). Spot-test disable alone: accounts 3.25→3.11 (-4.3%), notifications 7.90→8.14 (+3% variance). Requires monkey-patching gem (`module_eval`), global effect, small average gain. Skipped; webhook stub already removes webhook serializations, API response serializations are necessary and their `instrument` overhead is smaller than file I/O.
+- `Rails.logger.level=FATAL` for all (vs `AR.logger=nil`): silences Cache (1553 lines for accounts) + Chewy + ActionController in addition to SQL, log 1.6M→8.9K (-99%), but time 2.89→2.82 (-2% extra, within variance). Cache log file writes are cheap vs SQL. Kept `AR.logger=nil` only for consistency with models and to leave `Rails.logger` intact for any future assertions.
+- `Chewy.request_strategy=:bypass` for tests: `Mastodon#leave` self 1 sample (negligible) even though it does Redis `pipelined` per request when disabled. `Chewy.strategy` 21-37% total is wrapper, not overhead. Skipped.
+- `Rack::Attack` disable: in test, cache is `:memory_store` (not Redis), `IpBlock.blocked?` uses `Rails.cache.fetch` (DB once, then memory). Per-request overhead small. Skipped.
+- `Paperclip` stub for requests: only 10 files use `media_attachment`; those that do need real processing (`media_spec` asserts `have_attached_file`, `statuses_spec` DELETE asserts `url: %r{/system/media_attachments/files/}`, `credentials_spec` asserts `avatar: exist`). Previous models finding (2%, no gain for non-media) applies. Skipped.
+- Global `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` for requests (like models): would save Distribution/FanOut (17% in notifications) but break setup that needs inline side effects (`notifications_spec` needs `LocalNotificationWorker` inline to create `Notification` rows for `GET` to return; `timelines/home_spec` needs `Distribution` inline for feeds). Would require tagging 8+ files with `sidekiq_inline` and careful per-example analysis. Instead, kept `:inline` for requests and stubbed only unneeded side effects (webhooks, Notify emails). More minimal and safe.
+- `Faker`, `Bookmark`/`login_activity` cascades, `keypairs`: factory behavior, explicitly out of scope (“do not touch factories yet”). Webhook stub already mitigates cascade’s webhook part. Revisit in factories phase.
